every year scientists at the UN collate  all the different climate change  promises made by countries across the  planet and try to work out what that  will mean their new calculations have  just been released showing that while  there's been some improvement there's  still a long way to go  let's take temperature first of all  today the UN says that all the climate  commitments made so far will get us to  2.5 degrees Celsius of warming by 2050  has a very slight Improvement on last  year which was 2.7 degrees but this is  where we should be by 2050. limiting  warming to 1.5 degrees above  pre-industrial levels that's the best  case and would help us avoid the very  worst effects of climate change one  degree may not sound like much but it is  enough to dramatically increase the  impact of climate change on things like  sea level rises and severe events like  flooding drought and heat waves now  let's have a look at carbon dioxide  emissions another key metric here's  where we were last year after the UN  assessment predicted that emissions  would be up by 13.7 percent by 2030  compared to 2010 levels with emissions  continuing to rise there's actually been  an improvement this year current  commitments will increase emissions by  slightly less 10.6 percent by 20 30 and  will rise no further  but here's the bad news it's still  nowhere near enough this is where we  should be according to the UN emissions  need to be cut by 45 percent by 2030. if  we are to stay on track the 1.5 degrees  of warming so why do we have this huge  gap between what's been pledged and  what's needed well these dots represent  the 193 Nations that signed that famous  Paris climate agreement pledge to keep  global warming well below two degrees  and ideally at 1.5 last year at the cop  26 U.N climate Summit in Glasgow they  all promised to increase or update their  plans every single year well now we're  just a few weeks from the cop 27 climate  Summit in Egypt and only 24 countries  including the UK have submitted new  plans but lots of others like the U.S  and China have not  this all comes as the World  Meteorological organization warns that  atmospheric levels of carbon dioxide  nitrous oxide and especially Methane  have all reached record levels the world  leaders gathering in Egypt next month  there is a lot of work to do
 

